WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.813
SQL injection can let attackers change the meaning of database queries and access, modify, or delete records.

2
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:13.540
Any application feature that builds database statements from input needs strong query boundaries.

3
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:24.236
Command injection is especially severe because it can allow remote command execution through file processing, diagnostics, administrative utilities, or integration jobs.

4
00:00:24.236 --> 00:00:32.278
LDAP and NoSQL injection follow the same core pattern: attacker-controlled input changes the meaning of a query or filter.

5
00:00:32.278 --> 00:00:38.614
Identity, directory, search, and API features all require careful input handling.

6
00:00:38.614 --> 00:00:43.932
Input validation, parameterized operations, and output encoding work together.

7
00:00:43.932 --> 00:00:53.061
Validation rejects unexpected values, parameterization separates data from instructions, and encoding protects data when it is displayed.

8
00:00:53.061 --> 00:00:56.514
Use parameterized queries and safe APIs.

9
00:00:56.514 --> 00:00:59.966
Validate input against expected formats and ranges.

10
00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:03.987
Encode output for the correct context and avoid detailed error leakage.

11
00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:07.530
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include SI.

12
00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.967
L2-3.

13
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:09.865
14.

